<title>COTE D'IVOIRE: African robusta producers agree export cut shares.</title>
<headline>African robusta producers agree export cut shares.</headline>
<dateline>ABIDJAN 1997-02-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>African robusta coffee producers said on Friday they had agreed on how to share out a 850,000 bags cut in exports under a plan approved in January at a world producers' meeting in Brazil.</p>
<p>&quot;We have reached a common viewpoint on putting into place, for Africa, a mechanism for effective control for application of the export limit plan,&quot; said Interafrican Coffee Organization (IACO) chairman Guy-Alain Gauze.  </p>
<p>Gauze is Ivory Coast's commodities minister.</p>
<p>Africa's main robusta producers agreed to export limits for January to June 1997 expressed as a percentage of the overall 850,000 bags limitation as follows:</p>
<p>    Ivory Coast    29.00</p>
<p>    Uganda	   21.96</p>
<p>    Kenya	    12.08</p>
<p>    Ethiopia	 10.00</p>
<p>    Zaire	     7.86</p>
<p>    Cameroon	  7.05</p>
<p>    Tanzania	  6.92</p>
<p>    Madagascar	5.13</p>
<p>The deal is part of an Association of Coffee Producing Countries (ACPC) plan in January to cut world robusta exports about by a total of one million bags to prop up flagging prices.</p>
<p>ACPC groups the world's major coffee producers.</p>
<p>-- Matthew Bunce, Abidjan newsroom +225 21 90 90</p>
</text>
